    "case_id": "439",
    "case_summary": "A white woman in her 20s with unremarkable medical and ocular history presented with decreased vision in her right eye. Symptoms began 4 days prior to presentation, when she was struck by lightning directly to the right forehead while hiking.\n\n- Uncorrected visual acuity measured 20/50 OD and 20/20 OS- Pupils were symmetric, with no relative afferent pupillary defect- Initial anterior segment examination was normal with absence of cataract- Initial dilated fundus examination showed no apparent abnormalities in either eye- At 2 weeks: central macular thickening in fundus examination- At 7 weeks: mild posterior subcapsular cataract visible in right eye\n\nSpectral-domain OCT findings:- Initial examination (4 days post-injury):   Image Description: Preserved foveal contour in both eyes without cystoid abnormalities. Right eye showed subtle irregularity of the outer retinal layers, most notable nasal to the fovea  - Two weeks post-injury:  Image Description: Intraretinal cystoid changes and subfoveal ellipsoid zone disruption in the right eye  - Seven weeks post-injury:  Image Description: Restoration of foveal contour with decreased cystoid abnormalities, small subfoveal hyporeflective space, and further loss of outer retinal layers in the nasal macula  - Four months post-injury:  Image Description: Persistent subfoveal hyporeflective space in the inner retina with interval restoration of subfoveal outer retinal structures",
